representations.normalized:
Consider the task of multiparty quantum key agreement (MQKA): N spatially separated parties, connected by a quantum network with prescribed topology, wish to jointly establish a common secret key by sharing and locally measuring an N-partite entangled quantum resource state, possibly assisted by classical communication, in the presence of (i) photon loss along each channel modeled by independent bosonic pure-loss channels of transmissivity η, (ii) dephasing noise on each subsystem of rate γ, and (iii) device imperfections such as detector inefficiency, dark counts, and finite source visibility.

Standard resource families to consider include:
- N-qubit GHZ states |GHZ_N⟩ = (|0⟩^{⊗N} + |1⟩^{⊗N})/√2;
- N-qubit W states |W_N⟩ = (1/√N) Σ_i |0…1_i…0⟩;
- N-qubit cluster (and more general graph) states |G⟩ associated to a graph G;
- continuous-variable multimode squeezed (Gaussian) states such as CV-GHZ states obtained from squeezed vacua and beam-splitter networks;
- photonic graph states generated from single-photon or quantum-emitter sources;
- time-bin multiplexed entangled resources;
- "hybrid" states obtained as superpositions, tensor products, or continuous interpolations |Ψ(λ)⟩ = α(λ)|GHZ_N⟩ + β(λ)|W_N⟩ + … of the above families, including discrete-/continuous-variable hybrid encodings.

For each candidate resource family R, define performance functionals under the noise model above:
1. asymptotic secret-key rate K(R; η, γ, ε) per network use for an MQKA protocol;
2. output fidelity F(R; η) of the distributed state with its ideal target;
3. imperfection threshold ε*(R) (the maximal device-imperfection level for which K > 0);
4. generation/distribution scalability S(R, N), measured e.g. by the resource overhead required to produce R with fidelity ≥ 1 − δ over N nodes;
5. hardware-compatibility index H(R), encoding compatibility with available photonic, atomic, or superconducting platforms.

Problem. Identify and rigorously classify the multipartite resource families R — in particular hybrid families interpolating between GHZ, W, cluster/graph, CV squeezed, photonic graph, and time-bin multiplexed states — that strictly dominate the pure GHZ and pure W deployments in the MQKA setting above, in the sense that there exist nontrivial regimes of (N, η, γ, ε, network topology) where K(R) > max{K(|GHZ_N⟩), K(|W_N⟩)} while preserving comparable or better values of F, ε*, S, and H. Provide:
(a) explicit constructions of such dominant hybrid resources as a function of network topology and noise parameters;
(b) a classification theorem partitioning the resource landscape by the tuple (K, F, ε*, S, H);
(c) scalable measurement-based MQKA protocols for photonic graph states and time-bin multiplexed resources, together with their achievable key rates and loss/dephasing thresholds;
(d) tight upper bounds showing the limits of any such hybrid advantage.

